# Test JOIN-RANGE-7: the inner (right) side's range seek finds nothing
# at all -- departments.budget has no row past 10000 -- while the outer
# (left) side is a plain, unconditioned scan over all of courses.
#
# claude: this is the exact shape that caught a real bug while writing
# this feature: the initial SeekGt failing was (wrongly) routed to the
# *retry* address (the loop's own Next instruction) instead of the
# address *after* it. Next()/Prev() only degrade safely to "no more" for
# a not-yet-positioned cursor when the underlying array is empty (true
# for a Rewind failing on a genuinely empty table); a range Seek can
# fail on a perfectly non-empty index -- there just isn't an entry past
# the boundary -- so jumping into Next from that never-positioned state
# incorrectly stepped onto whatever entry sits at the start of the
# (unfiltered) array and silently resumed as if the walk were already
# underway, for every outer row. Caught by exactly this query producing
# one spurious extra output row instead of zero.

USE join-range-fixture.cdb

%%

SELECT title, name FROM courses NATURAL JOIN departments WHERE departments.budget > 10000;

%%

# No query results
